WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.444 --> 00:00:04.376
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.377 --> 00:00:08.065
All right, so
we talked about crowdsourcing,

00:00:08.066 --> 00:00:10.196
complaints, disasters.

00:00:10.197 --> 00:00:13.096
And I just mentioned
earlier it ultimately comes

00:00:13.097 --> 00:00:14.233
down to politics.

00:00:14.234 --> 00:00:16.915
And so this talk is going to
analyze the Indian political

00:00:16.916 --> 00:00:18.190
process.

00:00:18.191 --> 00:00:20.347
&gt;&gt; Well, a small part of it.

00:00:20.348 --> 00:00:25.540
Thank you Amed and [INAUDIBLE]
for inviting me here.

00:00:25.541 --> 00:00:28.150
So I'm the Ashoka University,

00:00:28.151 --> 00:00:32.025
it's a relatively new
University near Delhi.

00:00:32.026 --> 00:00:34.700
Where I co-direct a center
called Trivedi Center for

00:00:34.701 --> 00:00:35.597
Political Data.

00:00:35.598 --> 00:00:38.081
It's named after
Ashok Trivedi an early donor,

00:00:38.082 --> 00:00:40.630
who gave us money to fund
the center for ten years.

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:45.387
And we also have one of
my students is here,

00:00:45.388 --> 00:00:46.979
Saloni from Ashoka is here.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.534
So I'm presenting the work
of many many students and

00:00:49.535 --> 00:00:51.230
other faculty members and staff.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.447
So the center was started in
2016 by a couple of us from

00:00:57.448 --> 00:01:00.480
computer science and
political science, so

00:01:00.481 --> 00:01:02.092
it's a joint venture.

00:01:02.093 --> 00:01:05.002
I think it really worked very
well because when we started,

00:01:05.003 --> 00:01:07.340
I mean I didn't know
anything about politics.

00:01:07.341 --> 00:01:09.930
I think the political science
people didn't know much about

00:01:09.931 --> 00:01:12.370
data management, but we've
learned a lot from each other.

00:01:12.371 --> 00:01:14.100
So in that sense it's
been quite successful.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:20.470
The mission is to understand
Indian political processes and

00:01:20.471 --> 00:01:22.890
governmental processes
better through data.

00:01:22.891 --> 00:01:27.146
So one of our common TCs was
that there isn't enough informed

00:01:27.147 --> 00:01:28.657
discourse in India.

00:01:28.658 --> 00:01:31.548
So people would have a certain
theory about what's happening to

00:01:31.549 --> 00:01:33.742
cost equations or
what is happening to gender and

00:01:33.743 --> 00:01:34.663
so on across India.

00:01:34.664 --> 00:01:39.814
But even for something as
basic as a political process,

00:01:39.815 --> 00:01:45.631
there isn't good hard data that
you can base open end support.

00:01:45.632 --> 00:01:48.576
So we want to enable data
driven discourse through data.

00:01:48.577 --> 00:01:50.691
Part of this is of course
doing research ourselves.

00:01:50.692 --> 00:01:54.224
Part of it is organizing data
sets and providing them for

00:01:54.225 --> 00:01:57.157
journalists, for
students, researchers,

00:01:57.158 --> 00:02:01.950
whoever is interested in it and
disseminating that information.

00:02:01.951 --> 00:02:05.789
So this is all sort
of properly gathered,

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:09.420
somewhat teacher
quality check data.

00:02:09.421 --> 00:02:12.402
We are, of course,
strictly non-partisan and

00:02:12.403 --> 00:02:14.438
we have a research orientation,

00:02:14.439 --> 00:02:17.204
we collaborate with
several universities,

00:02:17.205 --> 00:02:20.350
typically in political
science across the world.

00:02:20.351 --> 00:02:22.910
We have 7 staff, about 20
students like I mentioned, and

00:02:22.911 --> 00:02:26.770
we're hiring, so if you have
students, post docs, people

00:02:26.771 --> 00:02:30.970
interested in this general
space do refer them to us.

00:02:30.971 --> 00:02:33.590
So I'll talk about a couple of
projects today the first one is

00:02:33.591 --> 00:02:39.680
called Lok Dhaba, it's a play
on Log Server obviously.

00:02:39.681 --> 00:02:43.474
This is a data set that you
can look at right now on our

00:02:43.475 --> 00:02:44.276
website.

00:02:44.277 --> 00:02:48.657
But what we have done is
essentially calculated

00:02:48.658 --> 00:02:53.045
Indian elections data
going back to 1962.

00:02:53.046 --> 00:02:56.970
And for all the states and
central elections until then

00:02:56.971 --> 00:02:59.955
we have gathered data,
integrated it and

00:02:59.956 --> 00:03:03.550
made it easy to visualize,
download, and so on.

00:03:03.551 --> 00:03:07.070
So this is one place where you
can find information about

00:03:07.071 --> 00:03:10.010
everyone who has contested in
election at the central or

00:03:10.011 --> 00:03:12.000
at the state level, we don't
go to the local level yet.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:16.415
So much of this data
actually is available of

00:03:16.416 --> 00:03:18.535
the Election Commission's
website,

00:03:18.536 --> 00:03:21.501
however it's not in formats
that are easy to consume.

00:03:21.502 --> 00:03:25.399
So for example, you would have
PDF files all over the place,

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:28.537
they'll be hiding
inconsistently organized or

00:03:28.538 --> 00:03:30.490
inconsistently formatted.

00:03:30.491 --> 00:03:34.370
They won't be in a way that you
can actually perform analysis

00:03:34.371 --> 00:03:34.984
on them.

00:03:34.985 --> 00:03:36.915
So we are trying to
solve that problem.

00:03:40.719 --> 00:03:43.500
There is no data quality
checking like I said.

00:03:43.501 --> 00:03:46.060
So they have legacy data which
they have collected over

00:03:46.061 --> 00:03:50.650
the ages but they really go back
and look at previous elections.

00:03:50.651 --> 00:03:53.779
So, one of the distinguished
fellows we have at this center

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:54.656
is Doctor S.Y.

00:03:54.657 --> 00:03:57.921
Quraishi the former Chief
Election Commissioner for India.

00:03:57.922 --> 00:04:01.988
So he has been very supportive
and helps to educate us about

00:04:01.989 --> 00:04:06.221
what the processes inside
the election commission are, and

00:04:06.222 --> 00:04:09.384
what the constraints and
capabilities are.

00:04:09.385 --> 00:04:10.704
So we have taken this data and

00:04:10.705 --> 00:04:12.800
we have made it available
in this interface.

00:04:12.801 --> 00:04:17.780
So just like you might
see on a news channel

00:04:17.781 --> 00:04:21.980
during elections, you can
generate your own graphs and

00:04:21.981 --> 00:04:25.680
maps of how various parties did.

00:04:25.681 --> 00:04:28.285
What was the turnout in
various constituencies?

00:04:28.286 --> 00:04:29.272
What was the gender ratio?

00:04:29.273 --> 00:04:31.961
Things like that.

00:04:31.962 --> 00:04:36.049
But you can ask this for any
election going back to 1962 and

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:39.120
you can sort of build
your own visualization.

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:46.370
So these are some maps, there
are some graphs, and so on.

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:51.760
But behind it all is a lot of
systematic quality checking of

00:04:51.761 --> 00:04:52.470
the data.

00:04:52.471 --> 00:04:55.658
So for a example, when we put
together all the election

00:04:55.659 --> 00:04:59.263
commission data, we find that
they are inconsistencies like

00:04:59.264 --> 00:05:02.269
same party, same election,
same constituency.

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:05.563
You have multiple people from
the same party which is not

00:05:05.564 --> 00:05:06.764
possible, right?

00:05:06.765 --> 00:05:10.023
So they have some data
entry errors somewhere or

00:05:10.024 --> 00:05:12.714
they have some problem
in the process.

00:05:12.715 --> 00:05:17.503
Inconsistent constituency type,
like constituency divided in

00:05:17.504 --> 00:05:21.681
India according to gender SC or
SD within the limitations

00:05:21.682 --> 00:05:24.654
that the type is not
supposed to change.

00:05:24.655 --> 00:05:28.945
But we find cases where they do
change, so these are a bunch of

00:05:28.946 --> 00:05:32.564
checks that our political
scientists thought of or

00:05:32.565 --> 00:05:36.140
we thought of and
included on this spreadsheet.

00:05:36.141 --> 00:05:39.000
So now that you have
a database of sorts,

00:05:39.001 --> 00:05:41.680
it's not actually
a very big database,

00:05:41.681 --> 00:05:43.680
in the sense of big data, it's
only about two million rows.

00:05:43.681 --> 00:05:46.180
So we're talking of all
candidates who ever contested,

00:05:46.181 --> 00:05:47.510
not just the winners.

00:05:47.511 --> 00:05:49.940
Everyone who's ever contested
an election at the state or

00:05:49.941 --> 00:05:52.990
the central level.

00:05:52.991 --> 00:05:56.630
So we load down these old
consistency checks to write and

00:05:56.631 --> 00:05:57.770
then we find these problems,

00:05:57.771 --> 00:06:00.250
we refer them back to the
election commission and so on.

00:06:00.251 --> 00:06:02.860
We also try to integrate
different sources, so

00:06:02.861 --> 00:06:08.190
things like whoever the election
commission deems to be a winner

00:06:08.191 --> 00:06:12.760
should correlate with whoever
ends up in local server, right?

00:06:12.761 --> 00:06:15.642
But we find that the Lok Sabha
record actually says something

00:06:15.643 --> 00:06:17.789
different, mostly its
a Lok Sabha problem,

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:20.505
actually the election
commission is relatively clean,

00:06:20.506 --> 00:06:22.510
data is relatively clean.

00:06:22.511 --> 00:06:27.632
But the Lok server has various
gotchas, various problems,

00:06:27.633 --> 00:06:32.154
sometimes there are processes
we don't know about or

00:06:32.155 --> 00:06:36.690
that are very kind of
mysterious or unknown process.

00:06:36.691 --> 00:06:40.411
Particularly in the earlier
years, so for example, and

00:06:40.412 --> 00:06:44.597
the reason we don't go beyond
1962 is that before then there

00:06:44.598 --> 00:06:48.413
were actually multiple winners
per seat, in some cases.

00:06:48.414 --> 00:06:53.634
Because some seats had special
members for special casts or

00:06:53.635 --> 00:06:57.610
even in some cases for
certain communities.

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:01.110
So it gets very very messy
before then but after 1962,

00:07:01.111 --> 00:07:02.550
it is relatively clean.

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:07.670
So I'll come back to this later
when I mention the kind of

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:12.294
work that computer science
research can enable over here.

00:07:12.295 --> 00:07:14.393
So this is
the Lok Sabha website.

00:07:14.394 --> 00:07:19.973
And the general problem is
given one data source and

00:07:19.974 --> 00:07:25.034
another data source how
do you map entries that

00:07:25.035 --> 00:07:29.989
are mostly similar and
integrated that way?

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:33.265
Another big problem is there
isn't a single identifier for

00:07:33.266 --> 00:07:35.240
a person in
the political process.

00:07:35.241 --> 00:07:39.810
So if you have even who was
contested in one election and

00:07:39.811 --> 00:07:42.210
in the next, you don't know
if that is the same person or

00:07:42.211 --> 00:07:44.660
not because it could be
a relatively common name.

00:07:44.661 --> 00:07:48.550
And the number of people with
very common names especially in

00:07:48.551 --> 00:07:52.410
states like Punjab and
is not funny.

00:07:52.411 --> 00:07:55.440
So because of this
political scientists

00:07:55.441 --> 00:07:58.190
can't ask questions that they're
very interested like what is

00:07:58.191 --> 00:08:00.730
happening with respect
to incumbency over time?

00:08:00.731 --> 00:08:03.290
Are there more people entering
the political process or less?

00:08:03.291 --> 00:08:05.343
How long do they stay on
average and things like that.

00:08:05.344 --> 00:08:10.626
What are the trends along age,
cast, gender, region?

00:08:10.627 --> 00:08:11.836
What are the differences
there and so on.

00:08:11.837 --> 00:08:16.224
So we've built what's
essentially an entity

00:08:16.225 --> 00:08:20.947
resolution tool to help map
people who are the same

00:08:20.948 --> 00:08:23.883
across different elections.

00:08:23.884 --> 00:08:26.550
And I'll skip
through it quickly.

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:29.900
Basically, if you look at the
literature entity resolution is

00:08:29.901 --> 00:08:31.510
kind of a solved problem,
but when you look for

00:08:31.511 --> 00:08:33.577
a tool that you can actually
give political scientists that

00:08:33.578 --> 00:08:34.844
they can use, there aren't many.

00:08:34.845 --> 00:08:38.829
So we have built a tool that
factors in approximate Indian

00:08:38.830 --> 00:08:42.406
name matching to help
resolve these rows and so on.

00:08:42.407 --> 00:08:44.965
So if you're interested come
talk to me later about it.

00:08:44.966 --> 00:08:48.054
I want to mention a second
project we did in the context of

00:08:48.055 --> 00:08:51.212
Smart Cities with the city
of Hubballi-Dharwad in north

00:08:51.213 --> 00:08:51.952
Karnataka.

00:08:51.953 --> 00:08:54.567
It so
happens that I lived there and

00:08:54.568 --> 00:08:57.370
I had some connections
to the city.

00:08:57.371 --> 00:09:00.060
And so when the Smart City
proposals were being worked out

00:09:00.061 --> 00:09:03.430
a couple of years ago,
I told them that ever other

00:09:03.431 --> 00:09:07.370
city was having a marathon, and
a workathon, and all of that.

00:09:07.371 --> 00:09:09.140
I said we'll have a hackathon.

00:09:09.141 --> 00:09:11.930
And so, because everyone
was interested in preparing

00:09:11.931 --> 00:09:16.370
the Smart City proposal, they
gladly gave a lot of data to us,

00:09:16.371 --> 00:09:17.440
which was very interesting.

00:09:17.441 --> 00:09:23.427
So we of course, we told
them about 5-Star Open Data,

00:09:23.428 --> 00:09:24.780
which [INAUDIBLE] has
been talking about.

00:09:24.781 --> 00:09:27.390
One star is when you
just put the data out,

00:09:27.391 --> 00:09:29.520
make it available without
the need for RTis and

00:09:29.521 --> 00:09:33.090
things like that to linked
open data which is 5-star and

00:09:33.091 --> 00:09:38.310
then it's integratable with
other data sources and so on.

00:09:38.311 --> 00:09:43.080
So a couple of my
students built this app,

00:09:43.081 --> 00:09:45.969
it's a site called hdworks.org,
you can go and check it.

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:50.675
Basically what it is it's a list
of all spending by the city,

00:09:50.676 --> 00:09:54.015
which is of course it's supposed
to be all public and RTI-able,

00:09:54.016 --> 00:09:56.142
except it's not
actually consumable and

00:09:56.143 --> 00:09:57.380
usable by most people.

00:09:57.381 --> 00:10:00.610
So you can in this case for
the city of Hubballi-Dharwad,

00:10:00.611 --> 00:10:02.020
you can look over all the works.

00:10:02.021 --> 00:10:05.750
You can see who's contractor,
what's the source of funding,

00:10:05.751 --> 00:10:09.140
what's the amount and so on.

00:10:09.141 --> 00:10:11.955
We have provided ways that you
can geotag, you can say this

00:10:11.956 --> 00:10:14.901
work was done on this road and
this much money was spent on it.

00:10:18.254 --> 00:10:21.217
You can have officials upload
pictures of the work that was

00:10:21.218 --> 00:10:24.422
done, so the officials will say
yeah this money was sanctioned

00:10:24.423 --> 00:10:26.130
for the road, that's the road.

00:10:26.131 --> 00:10:27.201
But there is also a way for

00:10:27.202 --> 00:10:29.802
citizens to give feedback and
then the citizens will tell you

00:10:29.803 --> 00:10:31.850
that the road is actually
something like this.

00:10:31.851 --> 00:10:34.399
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:37.563
this is very useful for actually
the municipal commissioner to

00:10:37.564 --> 00:10:39.779
look at what's happening
across the city.

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:41.567
So that's what we're
trying to enable and

00:10:41.568 --> 00:10:43.109
trying to study
crowd-sourcing and

00:10:43.110 --> 00:10:45.340
citizen involvement
in that context.

00:10:45.341 --> 00:10:47.530
We have sort of
standing queries, so

00:10:47.531 --> 00:10:50.970
you can subscribe to a certain
work or a certain ward.

00:10:50.971 --> 00:10:53.320
And get information when
there is an update about work

00:10:53.321 --> 00:10:56.140
happening in that ward and
so on.

00:10:56.141 --> 00:11:01.404
So I see I am out of time and
I'm going to skip to this last

00:11:01.405 --> 00:11:07.075
part to, I'll just take half

00:11:07.076 --> 00:11:10.310
a minute to mention computer
science problems in particular.

00:11:10.311 --> 00:11:14.320
So, easing data collection and
analysis from loosely

00:11:14.321 --> 00:11:17.520
structured sources, whether
it's web or other sources, it's

00:11:17.521 --> 00:11:20.980
a long-standing problem with
something that we experienced

00:11:20.981 --> 00:11:23.955
painfully over the last
couple of years.

00:11:23.956 --> 00:11:26.055
Automatic generation of
data quality checks so

00:11:26.056 --> 00:11:29.365
I mentioned a few data quality
checks that we thought about and

00:11:29.366 --> 00:11:31.715
enforced on
the election data set.

00:11:31.716 --> 00:11:35.445
But what if you could have tools
that sort of do data mining and

00:11:35.446 --> 00:11:37.505
online detection and
things like that, and

00:11:37.506 --> 00:11:40.995
figure out that this is a likely
check that two people from

00:11:40.996 --> 00:11:45.625
the same party can't contest in
same election, in the same year,

00:11:45.626 --> 00:11:47.080
in the same constituency.

00:11:47.081 --> 00:11:48.490
Can you join similar columns?

00:11:48.491 --> 00:11:50.090
Like I have
a Lok Sabha data set,

00:11:50.091 --> 00:11:51.870
I have an election
commission data set,

00:11:51.871 --> 00:11:54.680
they are mostly the same but
there are differences whether

00:11:54.681 --> 00:11:57.070
it's in spelling, or
for other reasons.

00:11:57.071 --> 00:11:58.730
Can I have a loose join,

00:11:58.731 --> 00:12:03.015
an interactive join where I can
say 90% of your data is okay,

00:12:03.016 --> 00:12:05.800
5% is okay, modular spelling
corrections and so on.

00:12:05.801 --> 00:12:07.390
5% is something you
need to look at,

00:12:07.391 --> 00:12:10.410
this is something that Soloni
for example spent a month on and

00:12:10.411 --> 00:12:12.180
can we automate this process?

00:12:12.181 --> 00:12:14.132
Can you have conversational
interfaces, data?

00:12:14.133 --> 00:12:17.538
So it seems like we can talk to
your phones, we can talk to our

00:12:17.539 --> 00:12:20.210
rooms and so on with Siri and
things like that.

00:12:20.211 --> 00:12:22.984
Can you instead have
political scientists or

00:12:22.985 --> 00:12:26.271
others talking to data
centers in natural language?

00:12:26.272 --> 00:12:30.464
Can express these whether
its quantity checks or

00:12:30.465 --> 00:12:33.790
queries in natural language?

00:12:33.791 --> 00:12:35.634
Okay and we are doing
a bunch of work on user

00:12:35.635 --> 00:12:36.645
interfaces and so on.

00:12:36.646 --> 00:12:40.473
So let's skip that, you can
contact me later if you have

00:12:40.474 --> 00:12:44.390
questions and also here today
and tomorrow, thank you.

00:12:44.391 --> 00:12:47.384
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

